+% Generated by roxygen2: do not edit by hand
+% Please edit documentation in R/dv_taxonomy_gbif.R
+\name{dv_taxonomy_gbif}
+\alias{dv_taxonomy_gbif}
+\title{Resolve prey to the GBIF Backbone Taxonomy (key, exact name, then fuzzy)}
+\usage{
+dv_taxonomy_gbif(
+  keys,
+  ranks = c("kingdom", "phylum", "class", "order", "family", "genus", "species"),
+  cache_dir = tools::R_user_dir("DietView", "cache"),
+  fuzzy = TRUE,
+  kingdom = NULL,
+  min_confidence = 90,
+  cache_max_age = 180,
+  verbose = TRUE
+)
+}
+\arguments{
+\item{keys}{Data.frame with columns \code{prey_id} (GBIF taxon key, may be \code{NA})
+and \code{prey_name} (may be \code{NA}); one row per unique prey. Other columns are
+kept.}
+
+\item{ranks}{Ranks to keep (default the seven flagship ranks). GBIF returns
+kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus and species; any other rank
+stays \code{NA}.}
+
+\item{cache_dir}{Directory for the on-disk cache. \code{NULL} disables caching.}
+
+\item{fuzzy}{If \code{TRUE} (default), accept fuzzy matches. \code{FALSE} asks GBIF
+for strict matching only.}
+
+\item{kingdom}{Optional kingdom hint passed to GBIF (e.g. \code{"Animalia"}),
+which helps with names shared by a plant and an animal genus.}
+
+\item{min_confidence}{GBIF confidence (0-100) below which a match is listed
+for review (default 90). The match is kept.}
+
+\item{cache_max_age}{Maximum age, in days, of a cached lookup (default 180).}
+
+\item{verbose}{Print progress.}
+}
+\value{
+\code{keys} with one column per rank plus \code{gbif_key}, \code{match_type}
+("id", "id_synonym", "id_unchecked", "id_fallback_name", "exact",
+"fuzzy", "higherrank", "unresolved"), \code{name_check}, \code{matched_name},
+\code{confidence}, \code{n_candidates} (exact-name candidates GBIF listed),
+\code{homonym}, \code{lineage_ok}, \code{retrieved} and \code{low_confidence}.
+}
+\description{
+The terrestrial and freshwater counterpart of \code{\link[=dv_taxonomy_worms]{dv_taxonomy_worms()}}: matches
+each unique prey to the GBIF Backbone Taxonomy, which covers all kingdoms,
+and returns its lineage for the declared ranks. It follows the same rules
+and returns the same provenance columns, so \code{\link[=dv_prepare]{dv_prepare()}},
+\code{\link[=dv_taxonomy_report]{dv_taxonomy_report()}} and the rest of the package treat both sources alike.
+Matching proceeds in this order:
+\enumerate{
+\item \strong{id} - a GBIF taxon key is already known (\code{prey_id}). Its record
+is read: a synonym is followed to its accepted taxon (\code{"id_synonym"})
+and the prey name stored with it is compared with the GBIF name
+(\code{name_check}). If the record cannot be read, the key is left
+unresolved for now (\code{"id_unchecked"}, \code{lineage_ok = FALSE}). A key GBIF
+does not know falls back to the prey name (\code{"id_fallback_name"});
+\item \strong{exact} - the GBIF species-match service finds the name as
+written;
+\item \strong{fuzzy} - (if \code{fuzzy = TRUE}) the service proposes a close name,
+which can rescue a misspelling;
+\item \strong{higherrank} - the name itself is unknown but a coarser taxon in it
+is (e.g. the genus of an unknown species); the lineage stops at that
+rank, so the record shows as unidentified below it. A step up to a
+kingdom alone (what GBIF answers for "Plastic" with \code{kingdom = "Animalia"}) is treated as no match;
+\item \strong{unresolved} - nothing matched; all ranks stay \code{NA}.
+}
+A name can belong to several taxa (Polychaeta is a class of worms and a
+genus of flies). GBIF then steps up to their shared ancestor, or gives no
+match. DietView looks at the alternatives GBIF lists: when exactly one of
+them is an exact match to an accepted name (in \code{kingdom}, if given), it is
+taken; when several are, the conservative GBIF answer is kept and the prey
+is flagged as a possible \code{homonym}, to be settled by a lineage table.
+A name matched as a synonym gets the classification of its accepted taxon.
+Every match type other than \code{"id"} and \code{"exact"}, a name that differs from
+the GBIF name, a match below \code{min_confidence} and a possible homonym are
+listed by \code{\link[=dv_taxonomy_report]{dv_taxonomy_report()}} for review.
+}
+\details{
+The GBIF Backbone has no class for some groups (ray-finned fishes, for
+instance, sit directly under Chordata). With \code{dv_prepare(gaps = "label")},
+the default, such a missing rank is filled as \code{"Chordata (no class)"}
+instead of looking unidentified.
+
+Needs internet access to \code{api.gbif.org} and no extra package. Successful
+lookups are cached to disk, in their own file next to the WoRMS cache, with
+the same expiry rules (\code{cache_max_age}); failed lookups are never cached and
+are retried on the next run, \code{getOption("DietView.gbif_retries", 3)} times
+per request with a 1, 2, 4 ... second backoff.
+}
+\examples{
+\dontrun{
+# needs internet access
+keys <- data.frame(prey_id   = c(NA, 8084280),
+                   prey_name = c("Crangon vulgaris", "Gadus morhua"))
+dv_taxonomy_gbif(keys)
+}
+}
+\seealso{
+\code{\link[=dv_taxonomy_worms]{dv_taxonomy_worms()}} for marine prey; \code{\link[=dv_parse_lineage]{dv_parse_lineage()}} when
+the lineage already comes with the data (e.g. from a metabarcoding
+pipeline).
+}
